+// ── CAPTURE TARGETS ───────────────────────────────────
+
+{ch:'capture',type:'mc',
+ q:`Given this <code>_capture</code>, what happens if you pass it a plain <code>str</code>?
+
+<pre class="snippet"><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_capture</span>(target) -> <span class="cls">QImage</span>:
+    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(target, <span class="cls">QWidget</span>):
+        <span class="kw">return</span> target.<span class="fn">grab</span>().<span class="fn">toImage</span>()
+    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(target, <span class="cls">QGraphicsItem</span>):
+        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">_capture_graphics_item</span>(target)
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">TypeError</span>(...)</pre>`,
+ opts:[
+   {t:'It raises a <code>TypeError</code> — only a <code>QWidget</code> or a <code>QGraphicsItem</code> is accepted.',correct:true,why:'Correct. Neither isinstance branch matches, so the explicit raise TypeError(...) fires, naming the wrong type.'},
+   {t:'It treats the string as a file path and loads it as the target image.',correct:false,why:'There is no path handling. A non-widget, non-item argument hits the TypeError branch.'},
+   {t:'It returns None and the comparison silently passes.',correct:false,why:'It raises rather than returning None — a misuse fails loudly, which is correct.'},
+   {t:'It grabs the active window as a fallback.',correct:false,why:'There is no fallback grab. The function strictly requires a QWidget or QGraphicsItem.'},
+ ]},
+
+{ch:'capture',type:'wr',
+ q:'Hardware blocks subclass <code>BaseBlock</code>. Why can\'t <code>compare_screenshot</code> capture a block with <code>block.grab()</code>, and what does it do instead?',
+ concepts:[
+   {label:'block is a QGraphicsItem not QWidget',terms:['qgraphicsitem','graphics item','not a qwidget','not qwidget']},
+   {label:'QGraphicsItem has no grab()/show()',terms:['no grab','no show','grab() does not','not have grab']},
+   {label:'rendered through QGraphicsScene.render',terms:['scene','render','qgraphicsscene','_capture_graphics_item']},
+ ],
+ model:'BaseBlock is a QGraphicsItem, not a QWidget. QGraphicsItem has no grab() and no show() — an item only exists inside a scene, it is not a standalone window. So _capture branches to _capture_graphics_item, which renders the item through a QGraphicsScene via scene.render(painter, ...).',
+},
+
+{ch:'capture',type:'wr',
+ q:`Read <code>_capture_graphics_item</code>:
+
+<pre class="snippet"><span class="kw">if</span> scene <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="pm">None</span>:
+    temp_scene = <span class="cls">QGraphicsScene</span>()
+    temp_scene.<span class="fn">addItem</span>(item)
+    scene = temp_scene
+...
+image.<span class="fn">fill</span>(<span class="cls">Qt</span>.<span class="cls">GlobalColor</span>.white)
+...
+<span class="kw">if</span> temp_scene <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="pm">None</span>:
+    temp_scene.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(item)</pre>
+
+Explain the temp-scene logic and why the background is filled white.`,
+ concepts:[
+   {label:'detached item needs a scene to render',terms:['detached','no scene','scene is none','needs a scene','only render']},
+   {label:'temp scene then remove to leave object untouched',terms:['remove','removeitem','untouched','restore','leave','side effect']},
+   {label:'white bg isolates block, not canvas grid',terms:['white','isolate','grid','just the block','alone','only the block']},
+ ],
+ model:'A QGraphicsItem can only be rendered through a scene. If item.scene() is None (a freshly constructed, detached block), there is nothing to render through, so the helper parks it in a throwaway QGraphicsScene, renders, then removeItem()s it so the caller object is left untouched (no side effects). The image is filled white so the golden captures just the block on a clean background — not the canvas grid or neighbouring blocks — keeping the block golden stable when the surrounding canvas changes.',
+},
+
+{ch:'capture',type:'mc',
+ q:'Which of these targets uses the <code>target.grab().toImage()</code> path, and which uses scene rendering?',
+ opts:[
+   {t:'MainWindow and <code>window.canvas_view</code> use grab(); each hardware block uses scene rendering.',correct:true,why:'Correct. MainWindow and CanvasView are QWidgets (grab path); blocks are QGraphicsItems (scene-render path).'},
+   {t:'Everything uses scene rendering — grab() is never used in these tests.',correct:false,why:'The main window and canvas view tests pass QWidgets, which take the grab() branch.'},
+   {t:'Everything uses grab() — blocks are wrapped in a QWidget proxy first.',correct:false,why:'Blocks are not wrapped in a proxy. They are QGraphicsItems and go through _capture_graphics_item.'},
+   {t:'Blocks use grab(); the window uses scene rendering.',correct:false,why:'Backwards. Widgets (window, canvas view) grab; items (blocks) render through a scene.'},
+ ]},
