+// ── DETERMINISM ───────────────────────────────────────
+
+{ch:'determinism',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why is the default threshold 5 rather than 0?',
+ opts:[
+   {t:'Font hinting and antialiasing differ between machines/Qt builds; 0 would be flaky, 5 absorbs sub-pixel noise but still catches real changes.',correct:true,why:'Correct — this is the documented rationale. A small RMS from antialiasing stays under 5; a moved label or recoloured header exceeds it.'},
+   {t:'PNG compression loses up to 5 levels per channel.',correct:false,why:'PNG is lossless. The tolerance exists for cross-machine antialiasing/font rendering differences, not compression.'},
+   {t:'5 means 5% of pixels may differ.',correct:false,why:'It is an RMS magnitude on the 0-255 scale, not a percentage of differing pixels.'},
+   {t:'Qt requires a minimum threshold of 5 for offscreen rendering.',correct:false,why:'Qt imposes no such requirement. 5 is a project choice to balance flakiness vs sensitivity.'},
+ ]},
+
+{ch:'determinism',type:'wr',
+ q:'The multimeter paints <code>base + random.gauss(0, noise)</code>. Why does that break a golden test, and write the helper that fixes it (name + what it patches).',
+ concepts:[
+   {label:'random reading differs every run',terms:['random','every run','different','never match','noise']},
+   {label:'_freeze_live_readings replaces _get_reading',terms:['_freeze_live_readings','_get_reading','freeze','replace','monkeypatch','lambda']},
+   {label:'returns FROZEN_READING constant',terms:['frozen_reading','constant','1.2345678','fixed value']},
+   {label:'hasattr guard skips devices without it',terms:['hasattr','getattr','guard','if hasattr']},
+ ],
+ model:'A fresh Gaussian-noise reading renders differently on every capture, so it could never match a golden (RMS would blow past 5). _freeze_live_readings(block) reads block.device, and if it has _get_reading it replaces it with a lambda returning the constant FROZEN_READING = 1.2345678. The hasattr guard means devices without _get_reading are left untouched. The block then paints a stable value, making its golden reproducible.',
+},
+
+{ch:'determinism',type:'mc',
+ q:'In <code>test_canvas_with_psu_block</code>, why is the window resized to a fixed 1000x700 before screenshotting?',
+ opts:[
+   {t:'So the canvas viewport geometry — and thus the grid and fitted block — render identically on every machine.',correct:true,why:'Correct. A fixed window size makes the viewport (and everything laid out in it) deterministic across environments; a default size would vary.'},
+   {t:'1000x700 is the only size the offscreen platform supports.',correct:false,why:'Offscreen rendering supports arbitrary sizes. The fixed size is for determinism, not a platform limit.'},
+   {t:'To keep the golden PNG file under a size limit.',correct:false,why:'There is no file-size limit driving this. It is about reproducible geometry.'},
+   {t:'Because fit_all() only works at 1000x700.',correct:false,why:'fit_all() works at any size. The fixed window size is independent of fit_all and exists for deterministic layout.'},
+ ]},
+
+{ch:'determinism',type:'wr',
+ q:'A new block is auto-placed off-screen at the far top-left of the scene. If you screenshot the canvas view immediately, the golden is wrong. What call fixes it and why, and what kind of wait is used to know the block exists first?',
+ concepts:[
+   {label:'fit_all() brings block into view',terms:['fit_all','fit all','show all','into view']},
+   {label:'otherwise empty viewport/grid in golden',terms:['empty','off-screen','offscreen','empty grid','not visible']},
+   {label:'qtbot.waitUntil not time.sleep',terms:['waituntil','wait_until','waitUntil','not sleep','no sleep']},
+ ],
+ model:'window.canvas_view.fit_all() (the app own "show all" action) changes the view transform so the auto-placed off-screen block is brought into the viewport; without it the golden would just show an empty grid. To know the block exists before fitting, the test uses qtbot.waitUntil(lambda: len(window.canvas.blocks) == 1, timeout=...) — never time.sleep — which blocks until the condition is actually true. Then qapp.processEvents() flushes paint events before the grab.',
+},
