+ model:'Qt selects its platform plugin from QT_QPA_PLATFORM when its modules are first imported. By the time the qapp fixture runs, PyQt6 has already been imported at the top of the test modules/conftest, so setting it there would be too late. The top-level conftest.py sets it before any Qt import, guaranteeing the offscreen plugin is chosen. Same "set env before import" ordering rule applies to many Qt knobs.',
+},
+
+{ch:'headless',type:'mc',
+ q:'What does the session-scoped <code>qapp</code> fixture do?',
+ opts:[
+   {t:'Returns the existing <code>QApplication.instance()</code> if there is one, else creates a single new <code>QApplication([])</code> for the whole session.',correct:true,why:'Correct: app = QApplication.instance() or QApplication([]); yield app. You can only have one QApplication, and session scope shares it across all tests.'},
+   {t:'Creates a fresh QApplication for every test function for isolation.',correct:false,why:'It is session-scoped, not function-scoped — and you can only ever have one QApplication. It reuses a single instance.'},
+   {t:'Sets QT_QPA_PLATFORM and then creates the QApplication.',correct:false,why:'The env var is set in the top-level conftest before import, not in qapp. qapp only constructs/reuses the application.'},
+   {t:'Starts xvfb and tears it down after each test.',correct:false,why:'xvfb is handled by pytest-xvfb automatically, not by the qapp fixture.'},
+ ]},
+
+// ── TRAPS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+
+{ch:'traps',type:'mc',
+ q:'A teammate "fixes" a flaky cross-platform visual test by setting <code>threshold=80</code> for that test. What is the real problem with this?',
+ opts:[
+   {t:'A threshold that large stops catching genuine layout/colour regressions — the test becomes a rubber stamp; the right fix is generating goldens on the target platform.',correct:true,why:'Correct. The doc says to generate goldens on the same platform the tests run on. Inflating the threshold to mask platform differences also masks real bugs.'},
