+<article>
+<h1>Visual Regression Testing</h1>
+<p class="why">Why this matters: the Silmulator GUI draws hardware faces (LCD readouts, waveform previews, port layouts) with raw <code>QPainter</code> calls. No unit assertion can tell you the multimeter's LCD shifted three pixels or the PSU header lost its colour. A golden-image test catches exactly that class of silent visual regression.</p>
+
+<!-- ══ §0 WHY ══ -->
+<section id="s0">
+<h2><span class="sn">§0</span> Why visual regression testing <button class="db" onclick="tick(0,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+
+<p>Ordinary tests assert on <em>values</em>: <code>assert window.windowTitle().startswith("Silmulator")</code>. They are blind to how something <em>looks</em>. A block can render with the wrong colour, a clipped label, or a misplaced port, and every value-based assertion will still pass.</p>
+
+<p>A <strong>visual regression test</strong> captures a screenshot of a GUI component and compares it pixel-for-pixel against a committed reference image — the <em>golden</em>. The test fails when the difference exceeds a threshold.</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>What it catches</th><th>Value assertion</th><th>Golden image</th></tr>
+  <tr><td>Wrong window title string</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="med">~</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Block header colour changed</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>LCD readout shifted / clipped</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Port moved to wrong edge</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Toolbar control disappeared after a paint change</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
+  <tr><td>Numeric simulation result</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The two are complementary. <code>test_gui_main_window.py</code> keeps both side by side: <code>test_main_window_title</code> asserts the title string, while <code>test_main_window_opens</code> calls <code>compare_screenshot(window, "main_window")</code> to guard the rendering.</p>
+
+<div class="key"><strong>Golden-image concept:</strong> the golden is the single ground truth of "what the UI is supposed to look like." It is committed to git. Every later test run re-renders the same component and diffs against it. A diff over threshold means either you broke something, or you changed the UI on purpose and need to refresh the golden.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §1 compare_screenshot ══ -->
+<section id="s1">
+<h2><span class="sn">§1</span> The compare_screenshot helper <button class="db" onclick="tick(1,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+<div class="fp">tests/visual/conftest.py</div>
+
+<p>One function does the whole job: <code>compare_screenshot(target, name, threshold=DEFAULT_THRESHOLD, update_golden=False)</code>. Its pipeline is <strong>capture → convert → compare → threshold</strong>.</p>
+
+<pre>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">compare_screenshot</span>(target, name: <span class="cls">str</span>,
+                       threshold: <span class="cls">int</span> = DEFAULT_THRESHOLD,
+                       update_golden: <span class="cls">bool</span> = <span class="op">False</span>) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
+    golden_path = GOLDEN_DIR / <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{name}.png"</span>
+    diff_path   = DIFF_DIR / <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{name}_diff.png"</span>
+    actual_path = DIFF_DIR / <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{name}_actual.png"</span>
+
+    update_golden = update_golden <span class="kw">or</span> os.environ.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="str">"UPDATE_GOLDEN"</span>, <span class="str">"0"</span>) == <span class="str">"1"</span>
+
+    actual = <span class="fn">_qimage_to_pil</span>(<span class="fn">_capture</span>(target))   <span class="cm">← capture + convert to PIL RGB</span>
+
+    <span class="kw">if</span> update_golden <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="kw">not</span> golden_path.<span class="fn">exists</span>():
+        GOLDEN_DIR.<span class="fn">mkdir</span>(exist_ok=<span class="op">True</span>)
+        actual.<span class="fn">save</span>(golden_path)            <span class="cm">← FIRST RUN: auto-create golden, pass</span>
+        <span class="kw">return</span>
+
+    golden = <span class="cls">Image</span>.<span class="fn">open</span>(golden_path).<span class="fn">convert</span>(<span class="str">"RGB"</span>)
+
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> actual.size == golden.size, ...   <span class="cm">← size mismatch is its own clear error</span>
+
+    diff = <span class="cls">ImageChops</span>.<span class="fn">difference</span>(actual, golden)
+    rms = <span class="fn">float</span>(np.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(np.<span class="fn">mean</span>(np.<span class="fn">asarray</span>(diff, dtype=<span class="fn">float</span>) ** <span class="num">2</span>)))
+
+    <span class="kw">if</span> rms > threshold:                      <span class="cm">← over threshold → FAIL + write diagnostics</span>
+        actual.<span class="fn">save</span>(actual_path)
+        amplified = <span class="cls">Image</span>.<span class="fn">fromarray</span>(
+            np.<span class="fn">clip</span>(np.<span class="fn">asarray</span>(diff) * <span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">255</span>).<span class="fn">astype</span>(np.uint8))
+        amplified.<span class="fn">save</span>(diff_path)        <span class="cm">← diff amplified 10x so it's visible</span>
+        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">AssertionError</span>(...)
+</pre>
+
+<h3>The four stages</h3>
+<div class="steps">
+  <span class="sn">capture</span>
+  <span class="st"><code>_capture(target)</code> returns a <code>QImage</code> — branching on whether the target is a <code>QWidget</code> or a <code>QGraphicsItem</code> (covered in §2).</span>
+  <span class="sn">convert</span>
+  <span class="st"><code>_qimage_to_pil()</code> converts the <code>QImage</code> to <code>Format_RGBA8888</code> first (known channel order, no row padding), wraps the buffer in a NumPy array, then returns a PIL image converted to <code>"RGB"</code>.</span>
+  <span class="sn">compare</span>
+  <span class="st"><code>ImageChops.difference(actual, golden)</code> produces a per-pixel difference image. The metric is <strong>RMS</strong>: <code>sqrt(mean(diff**2))</code> over every channel of every pixel.</span>
+  <span class="sn">threshold</span>
+  <span class="st">If <code>rms &gt; threshold</code> the test fails. <code>DEFAULT_THRESHOLD = 5</code>.</span>
+</div>
+
+<h3>Auto-create on first run</h3>
+<p>The key behaviour that makes this painless: <code>if update_golden or not golden_path.exists():</code> saves the screenshot as the golden and <code>return</code>s. So the very first time a new test runs, the golden is missing, it gets created, and the test passes. Every later run compares against it. You write the test once and get regression protection forever — you just commit the generated golden.</p>
+
+<h3>Diagnostics on failure</h3>
+<p>When the RMS exceeds threshold, two files land in <code>tests/visual/diffs/</code>: <code>&lt;name&gt;_actual.png</code> (what this run rendered) and <code>&lt;name&gt;_diff.png</code> (the difference, multiplied by 10 so a 2-pixel-value drift becomes visible to a human). The <code>AssertionError</code> message prints all three paths plus the exact <code>cp ... &amp;&amp; git add ...</code> command to accept the change if it was intentional.</p>
+
+<div class="note"><strong>Size mismatch is special.</strong> Before computing the RMS, the helper asserts <code>actual.size == golden.size</code>. <code>ImageChops.difference</code> requires equal dimensions, so a changed window/block size gets its own clear message ("Screenshot size changed: expected ..., got ...") instead of a confusing crash.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §2 TWO TARGETS ══ -->
+<section id="s2">
+<h2><span class="sn">§2</span> Capturing two kinds of targets <button class="db" onclick="tick(2,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+<div class="fp">tests/visual/conftest.py — _capture(), _capture_graphics_item()</div>
+
+<p>Silmulator's GUI has two fundamentally different visual objects, and they are captured differently:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Target</th><th>Examples</th><th>How captured</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>QWidget</code></td><td>MainWindow, CanvasView</td><td><code>target.grab().toImage()</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>QGraphicsItem</code></td><td>every hardware block</td><td>rendered through a <code>QGraphicsScene</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<pre>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_capture</span>(target) -> <span class="cls">QImage</span>:
+    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(target, <span class="cls">QWidget</span>):
+        <span class="kw">return</span> target.<span class="fn">grab</span>().<span class="fn">toImage</span>()        <span class="cm">← widgets can grab themselves</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">isinstance</span>(target, <span class="cls">QGraphicsItem</span>):
+        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">_capture_graphics_item</span>(target)
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">TypeError</span>(...)                     <span class="cm">← anything else is a usage error</span>
+</pre>
+
+<h3>Why blocks can't <code>.show()</code> / <code>.grab()</code></h3>
+<p>Hardware blocks (<code>MultimeterBlock</code>, <code>PowerSupplyBlock</code>, …) inherit from <code>BaseBlock</code>, which is a <code>QGraphicsItem</code> — <em>not</em> a <code>QWidget</code>. <code>QGraphicsItem</code> has no <code>show()</code> and no <code>grab()</code>: an item only exists inside a scene, it is not a standalone window. So <code>compare_screenshot(block, ...)</code> takes the scene path.</p>
+
+<h3>Rendering an item through a scene</h3>
+<pre>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_capture_graphics_item</span>(item: <span class="cls">QGraphicsItem</span>) -> <span class="cls">QImage</span>:
+    temp_scene = <span class="op">None</span>
+    scene = item.<span class="fn">scene</span>()
+    <span class="kw">if</span> scene <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="op">None</span>:                       <span class="cm">← detached item? park it in a temp scene</span>
+        temp_scene = <span class="cls">QGraphicsScene</span>()
+        temp_scene.<span class="fn">addItem</span>(item)
+        scene = temp_scene
+
+    source = item.<span class="fn">mapToScene</span>(item.<span class="fn">boundingRect</span>()).<span class="fn">boundingRect</span>()
+    width, height = <span class="fn">int</span>(source.<span class="fn">width</span>()), <span class="fn">int</span>(source.<span class="fn">height</span>())
+    image = <span class="cls">QImage</span>(width, height, <span class="cls">QImage</span>.<span class="cls">Format</span>.Format_ARGB32)
+    image.<span class="fn">fill</span>(<span class="cls">Qt</span>.<span class="cls">GlobalColor</span>.white)        <span class="cm">← white bg, NOT the canvas grid</span>
+
+    painter = <span class="cls">QPainter</span>(image)
+    painter.<span class="fn">setRenderHint</span>(<span class="cls">QPainter</span>.<span class="cls">RenderHint</span>.Antialiasing)
+    scene.<span class="fn">render</span>(painter, <span class="cls">QRectF</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0</span>, width, height), source)
+    painter.<span class="fn">end</span>()
+
+    <span class="kw">if</span> temp_scene <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="kw">not</span> <span class="op">None</span>:
+        temp_scene.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(item)             <span class="cm">← leave caller's object untouched</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> image
+</pre>
+
+<p>Three things to notice:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>White background, isolated item.</strong> The item is rendered alone on a white fill, so the golden captures just the block — not the canvas grid or neighbouring blocks. That keeps a block golden stable even when the canvas around it changes.</li>
+  <li><strong>Detached items are handled.</strong> If the block isn't in a scene yet (<code>item.scene() is None</code>), it is temporarily added to a throwaway <code>QGraphicsScene</code> for the render and removed afterward, so the caller's object is left exactly as it was.</li>
+  <li><strong>Bounding-rect crop.</strong> <code>source</code> is the item's bounding rect mapped to scene coordinates, so the captured image is exactly block-sized.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="note"><code>test_gui_blocks.py</code> uses the item path; <code>test_gui_main_window.py</code> (the window) and <code>test_gui_canvas.py</code> (<code>window.canvas_view</code>) use the widget path. Same helper, two branches.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §3 DETERMINISM ══ -->
+<section id="s3">
+<h2><span class="sn">§3</span> Determinism — making renders reproducible <button class="db" onclick="tick(3,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+
+<p>A golden test is only useful if the same component renders <em>identically</em> every run. Anything random, machine-dependent, or off-screen breaks that. Silmulator handles four sources of non-determinism.</p>
+
+<h3>1. Threshold = 5, not 0</h3>
+<p>Font hinting and antialiasing differ slightly between machines and Qt builds. A threshold of <code>0</code> (pixel-perfect) would make tests flaky across environments. <code>DEFAULT_THRESHOLD = 5</code> absorbs sub-pixel rendering noise while still catching real layout changes. The RMS is a per-channel value on the 0–255 scale, averaged over the whole image — so a small antialiasing wiggle stays under 5, but a moved label or recoloured header blows past it.</p>
+
+<h3>2. Freezing the multimeter's random reading</h3>
+<p>The multimeter device computes its reading as <code>base + random.gauss(0, noise)</code> — it paints a fresh, noisy value every time. That reading would differ on every capture and could never match a golden. <code>test_gui_blocks.py</code> monkeypatches it to a constant:</p>
+
+<pre>
+FROZEN_READING = <span class="num">1.2345678</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_freeze_live_readings</span>(block) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
+    device = <span class="fn">getattr</span>(block, <span class="str">"device"</span>, <span class="op">None</span>)
+    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">hasattr</span>(device, <span class="str">"_get_reading"</span>):
+        device._get_reading = <span class="kw">lambda</span> func=<span class="op">None</span>: FROZEN_READING
+</pre>
+
+<p>Any device that exposes <code>_get_reading</code> gets it replaced with a lambda returning <code>FROZEN_READING</code>. The block then paints a stable value, so its golden is reproducible. Devices without <code>_get_reading</code> are left untouched (the <code>hasattr</code> guard).</p>
+
+<h3>3. Fixed window size</h3>
+<p><code>test_gui_canvas.py</code> resizes the window to a constant <code>WINDOW_WIDTH = 1000</code>, <code>WINDOW_HEIGHT = 700</code> before screenshotting. The canvas viewport geometry — and therefore the background grid and the fitted block — then render identically on every machine. Without a fixed size, the golden would depend on whatever default size each environment chose.</p>
+
+<h3>4. fit_all() for off-screen blocks</h3>
+<p>The canvas auto-places a new block at the far top-left of the large scene, which is off-screen in the default view. If you screenshot the viewport then, the golden would just show an empty grid. The interaction test clicks the toolbar button, waits for the block, then calls the app's own <code>fit_all()</code> to bring it into view:</p>
+
+<pre>
+psu_button = <span class="fn">_find_palette_button</span>(window, <span class="str">"Power Supply"</span>)
+qtbot.<span class="fn">mouseClick</span>(psu_button, <span class="cls">Qt</span>.<span class="cls">MouseButton</span>.LeftButton)
+
+qtbot.<span class="fn">waitUntil</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span>: <span class="fn">len</span>(window.canvas.blocks) == <span class="num">1</span>, timeout=WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS)
+
+window.canvas_view.<span class="fn">fit_all</span>()        <span class="cm">← bring the auto-placed block into view</span>
+qapp.<span class="fn">processEvents</span>()
+compare_screenshot(window.canvas_view, <span class="str">"canvas_with_psu_block"</span>)
+</pre>
+
+<div class="warn"><strong>Never use <code>time.sleep()</code> to wait for the GUI.</strong> The tests use <code>qtbot.waitUntil(lambda: len(window.canvas.blocks) == 1)</code> and <code>qtbot.waitExposed(window)</code> — they block until the condition is actually true (or time out), which is both faster and not flaky. A fixed sleep is either too short (flaky) or too slow (wasteful). Also call <code>qapp.processEvents()</code> to flush pending layout/paint events before grabbing.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §4 UPDATE GOLDEN ══ -->
+<section id="s4">
+<h2><span class="sn">§4</span> The update-golden workflow <button class="db" onclick="tick(4,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+<div class="fp">docs/visual-testing.md</div>
+
+<p>When you change the UI <em>on purpose</em>, the old goldens no longer match and the tests fail — correctly. You then refresh the goldens. Two ways:</p>
+
+<pre>
+<span class="cm"># Update ONE golden: delete it and re-run; the helper auto-recreates it</span>
+rm tests/visual/golden/main_window.png
+pytest tests/visual/test_gui_main_window.py::test_main_window_opens
+
+<span class="cm"># Update EVERY golden at once via the env var</span>
+UPDATE_GOLDEN=<span class="num">1</span> pytest tests/visual/
+</pre>
+
+<p>Both routes go through the same branch in <code>compare_screenshot</code>: <code>if update_golden or not golden_path.exists()</code>. Deleting the file makes <code>exists()</code> false; <code>UPDATE_GOLDEN=1</code> sets the flag directly. Either way the current render is saved as the new golden and the test passes.</p>
+
+<div class="warn"><strong>Review before you commit.</strong> A golden update is only correct if the new rendering is what you intended. Open <code>&lt;name&gt;_actual.png</code> (or the regenerated golden) and eyeball it. A blindly accepted golden silently bakes in whatever regression you were supposed to catch — the test will then "pass" forever on the bug.</div>
+
+<h3>What's committed vs ignored</h3>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Path</th><th>Committed?</th><th>Role</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>tests/visual/golden/</code></td><td class="yes">✓ committed</td><td>ground truth references</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>tests/visual/diffs/</code></td><td class="no">✗ git-ignored</td><td>per-run diagnostics (actual + amplified diff)</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<pre>
+git add tests/visual/golden/
+git commit -m <span class="str">"chore(visual): update goldens after &lt;describe UI change&gt;"</span>
+</pre>
+
+<div class="key">Mental model: goldens are <em>source</em> — they live in git and change deliberately through review. Diffs are <em>artifacts</em> — generated on failure, never committed, safe to delete anytime.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §5 HEADLESS ══ -->
+<section id="s5">
+<h2><span class="sn">§5</span> Headless execution <button class="db" onclick="tick(5,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+
+<p>These are GUI tests, but they run with <strong>no display</strong> — locally, in CI, and on the overnight EC2 runner. You never set <code>DISPLAY</code> by hand and never prefix with <code>xvfb-run</code>. Two pieces make that work.</p>
+
+<h3>1. QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen</h3>
+<p>The repository's top-level <code>tests/conftest.py</code> sets <code>QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen</code> <em>before any Qt module is imported</em>. That tells Qt to render to an in-memory surface instead of a real window server. Ordering matters: the variable must be set before Qt reads it at import time, which is exactly why it lives in the top-level conftest rather than inside a test.</p>
+
+<p>The session-scoped <code>qapp</code> fixture then just constructs the single application:</p>
+<pre>
+<span class="at">@pytest.fixture</span>(scope=<span class="str">"session"</span>)
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">qapp</span>():
+    app = <span class="cls">QApplication</span>.<span class="fn">instance</span>() <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="cls">QApplication</span>([])
+    <span class="kw">yield</span> app
+</pre>
+<p>One <code>QApplication</code> for the whole session (you can only have one), reused if some other fixture already made one.</p>
+
+<h3>2. pytest-xvfb on EC2</h3>
+<p>On a fresh headless Linux box, <code>pytest-xvfb</code> additionally starts a virtual X framebuffer automatically when the package is installed (it's in <code>requirements-dev.txt</code>). Combined with offscreen rendering, that means the full GUI suite runs with a plain:</p>
+<pre>
+pytest tests/ -v          <span class="cm"># visual tests included automatically, no DISPLAY</span>
+</pre>
+
+<p>A one-time <code>./scripts/setup-visual-testing.sh</code> installs the system-level <code>xvfb</code> and Qt XCB runtime libraries via <code>apt-get</code>. After that, every checkout just needs <code>pip install -r requirements-dev.txt</code>.</p>
+
+<div class="note"><strong>Generate goldens on the same platform the tests run on.</strong> Because rendering differs slightly between platforms, goldens created on the EC2 runner match most closely there. If you generate a golden on macOS and run the test on Linux, even threshold=5 may not absorb the difference.</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ══ §6 PRACTICE ══ -->
+<section id="s6">
+<h2><span class="sn">§6</span> Practice <button class="db" onclick="tick(6,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
+
+<div class="quiz">
+<h3>Quick-fire quiz</h3>
+
+<!-- Q1 -->
+<div class="qblock" data-q="1">
+<div class="qt">Q1. The first time a brand-new visual test runs, no golden file exists yet. What does <code>compare_screenshot</code> do?</div>
+<ul class="opts">
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="It does not fail. The whole point of the auto-create branch is that a new test passes once so you can adopt visual testing without a manual bootstrap step.">It raises an AssertionError saying the golden is missing, so you must generate it manually first.</li>
+  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. The branch `if update_golden or not golden_path.exists()` saves the current render to golden/<name>.png and returns. The test passes; you then commit the new golden.">It saves the current screenshot as <code>golden/&lt;name&gt;.png</code>, prints a message, and returns — the test passes.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no skip. Missing golden is treated as 'first run', which auto-creates the golden and passes — it does not skip.">It skips the test with pytest.skip until a golden has been committed.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="It compares against nothing only in the sense that it can't — there is no golden. Instead it creates one. RMS is never computed on the first run.">It compares the render against an all-white blank image and passes if the block is mostly white.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="fb" id="fb1"></div>
+</div>
+
+<!-- Q2 -->
+<div class="qblock" data-q="2">
