+<!-- Q2 -->
+<div class="qblock" data-q="2">
+<div class="qt">Q2. A hardware block is a <code>QGraphicsItem</code>, not a <code>QWidget</code>. Why can't <code>compare_screenshot</code> just call <code>block.grab()</code> like it does for the main window?</div>
+<ul class="opts">
+  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. QGraphicsItem has no grab() or show() — an item only exists inside a scene, it is not a standalone window. So _capture branches to _capture_graphics_item, which renders the item through a QGraphicsScene.">A <code>QGraphicsItem</code> has no <code>grab()</code> (or <code>show()</code>) method — it only exists inside a scene, so it is rendered via <code>QGraphicsScene.render()</code> instead.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="grab() is not slow on items — it simply does not exist on QGraphicsItem. The reason is API availability, not performance.">Blocks have a <code>grab()</code> method but it is too slow, so the scene-render path is used for speed.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="grab() does not produce transparent output for items — items have no grab() at all. The capture uses a white fill deliberately, but that's a separate design choice.">Blocks' <code>grab()</code> returns a transparent image, so a white background must be added by rendering through a scene.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="The difference is real and structural: QWidget vs QGraphicsItem are different Qt base classes with different APIs. grab() belongs to QWidget only.">There is no real difference — both call <code>grab()</code>; <code>_capture_graphics_item</code> is just a thin alias.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="fb" id="fb2"></div>
+</div>
