+<!-- Q3 -->
+<div class="qblock" data-q="3">
+<div class="qt">Q3. Why is <code>DEFAULT_THRESHOLD = 5</code> instead of <code>0</code> (pixel-perfect)?</div>
+<ul class="opts">
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="The threshold is not about block colour ranges. It is the max allowed RMS pixel difference, chosen to absorb cross-machine rendering noise.">Blocks use colours within 5 units of each other, so anything tighter would confuse adjacent shades.</li>
+  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. Font hinting and antialiasing differ slightly between machines and Qt builds. Threshold 0 would make tests flaky everywhere; 5 absorbs sub-pixel noise while still catching real layout/colour changes.">Font hinting and antialiasing differ between machines/Qt builds; threshold 0 would be flaky, while 5 absorbs sub-pixel noise but still catches real changes.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="The threshold is a difference tolerance, not a percentage of pixels. RMS is sqrt(mean(diff**2)) across all channels of all pixels.">5 means 5% of pixels are allowed to differ completely before the test fails.</li>
+  <li data-correct="false" data-why="JPEG is never involved — goldens are saved as PNG, which is lossless. The tolerance exists for antialiasing/font differences, not compression artifacts.">PNG compression introduces up to 5 units of loss, so the threshold compensates for it.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="fb" id="fb3"></div>
+</div>
