+<h3 style="margin-top:2rem;color:#e6edf3">Scenario questions</h3>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">You add a new <code>SignalGeneratorBlock</code> and want a visual test for it. Walk through what you write, what happens on the first <code>pytest</code> run, and what you commit.</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">Add the block to BUILTIN_BLOCKS in test_gui_blocks.py mapping its registry key to a golden stem (e.g. "Signal Generator": "signal_generator_block"). The parametrised test_block_renders instantiates it, calls _freeze_live_readings, and calls compare_screenshot(block, "signal_generator_block"). First run: golden/signal_generator_block.png doesn't exist, so compare_screenshot saves the render and returns — test passes. You then OPEN the generated PNG to confirm it looks right, and git add tests/visual/golden/signal_generator_block.png. Every later run diffs against it.</div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">A teammate's multimeter visual test passes on their Mac but fails on the EC2 runner with RMS just above 5. Diagnose and give the fix.</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">The golden was generated on macOS but the test runs on Linux; font hinting and antialiasing differ between platforms, pushing the RMS just over the threshold of 5. The fix is to generate goldens on the same platform the tests run on — regenerate the golden on the EC2 runner (UPDATE_GOLDEN=1 pytest, or delete + rerun there), review it, and commit that version. (Do NOT just raise the threshold — that weakens regression protection. The doc explicitly says to generate goldens on the target platform.)</div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">Explain, in order, the four stages <code>compare_screenshot</code> performs on a normal (golden-exists) run, naming the key function or operation at each stage.</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">(1) Capture: _capture(target) → QImage, branching on QWidget (target.grab().toImage()) vs QGraphicsItem (_capture_graphics_item via QGraphicsScene.render). (2) Convert: _qimage_to_pil() converts to Format_RGBA8888, wraps the buffer in a NumPy array, returns a PIL "RGB" image. (3) Compare: assert sizes equal, then ImageChops.difference(actual, golden) and rms = sqrt(mean(diff**2)). (4) Threshold: if rms > threshold, save actual + 10x-amplified diff into diffs/ and raise AssertionError with the cp/git-add hint; otherwise pass.</div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">Why does <code>_capture_graphics_item</code> create a temporary <code>QGraphicsScene</code> when <code>item.scene()</code> is <code>None</code>, and why does it remove the item again at the end?</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">A QGraphicsItem can only be rendered through a scene — scene.render() does the painting. If the block isn't attached to a scene yet (item.scene() is None, e.g. it was just constructed by the parametrised test), there's nothing to render through, so the helper parks it in a throwaway QGraphicsScene. It removes the item again (temp_scene.removeItem(item)) so the caller's object is left exactly as it was found — no surprise side effects of having been captured. Items that already belong to a scene are rendered in place and not removed.</div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">Your CI shows a visual failure. The artifacts you need to diagnose it live in <code>tests/visual/diffs/</code>, but that directory is git-ignored. How do you actually look at them, and which file shows you <em>where</em> the change is?</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">diffs/ is git-ignored because it's per-run diagnostic output, not source — so it won't be in the repo, you retrieve it from the CI job's artifacts (or reproduce the failure locally and look in tests/visual/diffs/). Two files are written on failure: &lt;name&gt;_actual.png (what this run rendered) and &lt;name&gt;_diff.png (the per-pixel difference amplified 10x). The amplified _diff.png is the one that shows WHERE they differ — small drifts become visibly bright. Compare _actual.png against the committed golden/&lt;name&gt;.png side by side to see what changed.</div>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="sc-q">Why must <code>QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen</code> be set in the top-level <code>tests/conftest.py</code> rather than inside the <code>qapp</code> fixture?</div>
+  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
+  <div class="sc-ans">Qt reads QT_QPA_PLATFORM when its modules are first imported and the platform plugin is selected. By the time the qapp fixture runs, Qt (PyQt6) has already been imported — at the top of the test modules and conftest — so setting the variable there would be too late. The top-level conftest.py runs/sets it before any Qt import happens, guaranteeing Qt picks the offscreen platform plugin. This is the same 'set env before import' ordering rule that governs many Qt configuration knobs.</div>
+</div>
+</section>
+
